{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "Steel Gate Valves—Flanged and Butt-welding Ends, Bolted Bonnets"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "1 Scope"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This standard specifies the requirements for a heavy-duty series of bolted bonnet steel gate valves for petroleum refinery and related applications where corrosion, erosion, and other service conditions would indicate a need for full port openings, heavy wall sections, and large stem diameters."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This standard sets forth the requirements for the following gate valve features:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— bolted bonnet,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— outside screw and yoke,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— rising stems,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— non-rising handwheels,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— single or double gate,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— wedge or parallel seating,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— metallic seating surfaces,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— flanged or butt-welding ends."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "It covers valves of the nominal pipe sizes DN:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— 25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 650, 700, 750, 800, 850, 900, 950, 1000, 1050;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "corresponding to nominal pipe sizes NPS:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— 1, 1 1/4, 1 1/2, 2, 2 1/2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 24, 26, 28, 30, 32, 34, 36, 38, 40, 42;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "and applies to pressure class designations:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— 150, 300, 600, 900, 1500, 2500."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If product is supplied bearing the API Monogram and manufactured at a facility licensed by API, the requirements of Annex A apply."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "2 Normative References"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The following referenced documents are indispensable for the application of this document. For dated references, only the edition cited applies. For undated references, the latest edition of the referenced document (including any amendments) applies."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "API Standard 598, Valve Inspection and Testing"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "API Standard 624, Type Testing of Rising Stem Valves Equipped with Graphite Packing for Fugitive Emissions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B1.1 ¹, Unified Inch Screw Threads (UN and UNR Thread Form)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "¹ ASME International, 2 Park Avenue, New York, New York 10016-5990, www.asme.org."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "Terms and Definitions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For the purposes of this document, the following definitions apply."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "Class"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An alphanumeric designation that is used for reference purposes relating to valve pressure/temperature capability, taking into account valve material mechanical properties and valve dimensional characteristics. It comprises \"Class\" followed by a dimensionless whole number. The number following \"Class\" does not represent a measurable value and is not used for calculation purposes except where specified in this standard. The allowable pressure for a valve"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B1.5, Acme Screw Threads"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B1.8, Stub Acme Screw Threads"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B1.12, Class 5 Interference—Fit Thread"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B1.13M, Metric Screw Threads: M Profile"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B16.5, Pipe Flanges and Flanged Fittings NPS 1/2 through NPS 24 Metric/Inch"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B16.10, Face-to Face and End-to-End Dimensions of Valves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B16.25, Butt-welding Ends"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B16.34, Valves—Flanged, Threaded and Welding End"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B16.47, Large Diameter Steel Flanges, NPS 26 Through NPS 60 Metric/Inch Standard"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B18.2.2, Nuts for General Applications: Machine Screw Nuts, Hex, Square, Hex Flange, and Coupling Nuts (Inch Series)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B18.2.6M, Metric Fasteners for Use in Structural Applications"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ASME B31.3, Process Piping"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ISO 5210 ², Industrial valves—Multi-turn valve actuator attachments"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ISO 5752, Metal valves for use in flanged pipe systems—Face-to-face and centre-to-face dimensions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "ISO 15649, Petroleum and natural gas industries—Piping"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "MSS SP-91 ³, Guidelines for Manual Operation of Valves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "MSS SP-102, Multi-Turn Valve Actuator Attachment—Flange and Driving Component Dimensions and Performance Characteristics"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "MSS SP-144, Pressure Seal Bonnet Valves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "2 International Organization for Standardization, 1, ch. de la Voie-Creuse, Case postale 56, CH-1211, Geneva 20, Switzerland, www.iso.org."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "3 Manufacturers Standardization Society of the Valve and Fittings Industry, Inc., 127 Park Street, NE, Vienna, Virginia 22180-4602, www.mss-hq.com."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "having a class number depends on the valve material and its application temperature and is to be found in tables of pressure/temperature ratings."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "3.2 DN"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An alpha numeric designation of size that is common for components used in a piping system, used for reference purposes, comprising the letters \"DN\" followed by a dimensionless number indirectly related to the physical size of the bore or outside diameter of the end connection as appropriate. The dimensionless number following \"DN\" does not represent a measurable value and is not used for calculation purposes except where specified."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "3.3 NPS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An alpha numeric designation of size that is common for components used in a piping system, used for reference purposes, comprising the letters \"NPS\" followed by a dimensionless number indirectly related to the physical size of the bore or outside diameter of the end connection as appropriate. The dimensionless number may be used as a valve size identifier without the prefix \"NPS.\" The dimensionless size identification number does not represent a measurable value and is not used for calculation purposes."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "3.4 Shell"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Comprised of the body, bonnet and body-bonnet bolting which constitute the pressure boundary of an API 600 valve."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "4 Pressure/Temperature Ratings"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "4.1 Pressure/temperature ratings shall be in accordance with those specified in the tables of ASME B16.34 for standard class for the applicable material specification and the applicable class."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "4.2 Restrictions of temperature and concurrent pressure, or pressure and concurrent temperature, (e.g. those imposed by special soft seals or special trim materials), shall be marked on the valve identification plate (see Section 8)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "4.3 The temperature for a corresponding pressure rating is the maximum temperature of the pressure-containing shell of the valve. In general, this temperature is the same as that of the contained fluid. The use of a pressure rating corresponding to a temperature other than that of the contained fluid is the responsibility of the user."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "4.4 For temperatures below the lowest temperature listed in the pressure/temperature tables, the service pressure shall be no greater than the pressure for the lowest listed temperature. The use of valves at lower temperatures is the responsibility of the user. Consideration should be given to the loss of ductility and impact strength of many materials at low temperature."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "4.5 Double seated valves, in some design configurations, may be capable of trapping liquid in the center cavity of the valve when in the closed position. If subjected to an increase in temperature, an excessive build-up of pressure can occur, which may result in a pressure boundary failure. Where such a condition is possible, it is the responsibility of the user to provide or require to be provided, means in design, installation, or operating procedure, to assure that the pressure in the valve does not exceed that allowed by this standard for the resultant temperature."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "5 Design"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.1 Body Wall Thickness"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.1.1 A valve body schematic is shown as Figure 1. The minimum body wall thickness, tm, at the time of manufacture shall be as given in Table 1, except as indicated in 5.1.2 for butt-welding valve ends. Additional metal thickness needed for assembly stresses, stress concentrations, and shapes other than circular shall be determined by individual manufacturers, since these factors vary widely."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "description": "Схема с обозначениями частей клапана. Цифры 1-9 указывают на: 1 - соединение корпуса и шейки; 2 - фланец конца корпуса; 3 - внутренний диаметр порта конца корпуса; 4 - ось шейки корпуса; 5 - фланец корпуса/боннета; 6 - шейка корпуса; 7 - ось корпуса; 8 - сварной конец; 9 - корпус. Подписью под схемой: 'Figure 1—Identification of Terms'."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.1.2 The weld end preparation in butt-welding end valves (see 5.3.2) shall not reduce the body wall thickness to less than the values specified in 5.1.1 within a region closer than t_m to the outside surface of the body neck, measured along the run direction. The transition to the weld preparation shall be gradual and the section shall be essentially circular through the entire length of the transition. Sharp discontinuities or abrupt changes in section in areas that infringe into the transition shall be avoided, except that test collars or bands, either welded or integral, are allowed. In no case shall the thickness be less than 0.77t_m at a distance of 2t_m from the weld end."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.2 Bonnet Wall Thickness"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The minimum bonnet wall thickness at the time of manufacture, except for the neck extension that contains the packing, shall be t_m as given in Table 1. For the neck extension, the local minimum wall thickness shall be based on the local diameter, e.g. the inside diameter of the stem bore or packing box bore, and shall be in accordance with the valve body neck rules of ASME B16.34."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.3 Body Dimensions"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.3.1 Flanged Ends"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.3.1.1 Body end flanges shall comply with the dimensional requirements of ASME B16.5 for sizes up to and including DN 600 (NPS 24). For sizes over DN 600 (NPS 24), body end flanges shall comply with the dimensional requirements of ASME B16.47 Series A or Series B as specified by the purchaser. Unless otherwise specified, raised face end flanges shall be provided. The purchaser may specify a flange facing finish other than that specified in ASME B16.5 or ASME B16.47, as applicable."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.3.1.2 Face-to-face dimensions shall be in accordance with ASME B16.10 or ISO 5752. For sizes not listed, dimensions shall be as agreed to between purchaser and manufacturer. Body end flanges and bonnet flanges shall be cast or forged integral with the body. However, flanges may be attached by welding when approved by the purchaser."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.3.1.2.1 Welding a flange to a valve body shall be by full penetration butt-welding. Unless otherwise specified, attachment weld shall conform to ASME B31.3 or ISO 15649 for normal fluid service, including weld quality acceptance criteria and qualifications for the weld procedure and welder or welding operator. Heat treatment shall be performed in accordance with Table 2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.3.1.2.2 Integral or other alignment rings (centering backing rings) used to facilitate welding shall be removed after the weld is completed."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.3.2 Butt-welding Ends"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.3.2.1 Butt-welding ends for valve sizes greater than NPS 2 shall conform to the requirements of ASME B16.25 for the bore specified for use without backing rings. Butt-welding ends for valves DN 50 (NPS 2) and smaller shall conform to the requirement of API 602. Conversion of a flanged end valve to a butt-welding valve is not permitted except by agreement between the purchaser and manufacturer."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The chemical composition of carbon steel welding ends shall meet the following requirements unless otherwise agreed:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— the carbon content shall not exceed 0.23 % by mass;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— the carbon equivalent, CE, shall not exceed 0.43 as determined by the following formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "CE = C + Mn/6 + (Cr + Mo + V)/5 + (Ni + Cu)/15"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "where"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "C is weight % Carbon"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Mn is weight % Manganese."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Table 2—Post Weld Heat Treatment for Flange to Body Weld",
      "columns": [
        "Material",
        "Thickness t mm (in.)",
        "Temperature Range °C (°F)",
        "Holding Time/Thickness minutes/millimeter (hours/inch)",
        "Weld Hardness HBN Max"
      ],
      "rows": [
        {
          "Material": "Carbon steels",
          "Thickness t mm (in.)": "t > 19 (0.75)",
          "Temperature Range °C (°F)": "593 to 649 (1100 to 1200)",
          "Holding Time/Thickness minutes/millimeter (hours/inch)": "2.4 (1) minimum time 1 hr.",
          "Weld Hardness HBN Max": "—"
        },
        {
          "Material": "Alloy steels: 1/2 % < Cr ≤ 2 %",
          "Thickness t mm (in.)": "t > 13 (0.50)",
          "Temperature Range °C (°F)": "704 to 746 (1300 to 1375)",
          "Holding Time/Thickness minutes/millimeter (hours/inch)": "2.4 (1) minimum time 2 hr.",
          "Weld Hardness HBN Max": "225"
        },
        {
          "Material": "2 1/4 % ≤ Cr ≤ 10 %",
          "Thickness t mm (in.)": "All",
          "Temperature Range °C (°F)": "704 to 760 (1300 to 1400)",
          "Holding Time/Thickness minutes/millimeter (hours/inch)": "2.4 (1) minimum time 2 hr.",
          "Weld Hardness HBN Max": "241"
        },
        {
          "Material": "Nickel alloy steels",
          "Thickness t mm (in.)": "t > 19 (0.75)",
          "Temperature Range °C (°F)": "593 to 635 (1100 to 1175)",
          "Holding Time/Thickness minutes/millimeter (hours/inch)": "1.2 (1 1/2) minimum time 1 hr.",
          "Weld Hardness HBN Max": "—"
        },
        {
          "Material": "Austenitic steels a b",
          "Thickness t mm (in.)": "All",
          "Temperature Range °C (°F)": "solution anneal per the material specification",
          "Holding Time/Thickness minutes/millimeter (hours/inch)": "",
          "Weld Hardness HBN Max": ""
        },
        {
          "Material": "Other materials",
          "Thickness t mm (in.)": "All",
          "Temperature Range °C (°F)": "per the material specification",
          "Holding Time/Thickness minutes/millimeter (hours/inch)": "",
          "Weld Hardness HBN Max": ""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "a Thickness, t, is the greater thickness of the pieces being joined by welding."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "b Except when materials being welded are L-Grades or stabilized grades."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Cr is weight % Chromium"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Mo is weight % Molybdenum"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "V is weight % Vanadium"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Ni is weight % Nickel"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Cu is weight % Copper"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.3.2.2 End-to-end dimensions for butt-welding end class designated valves shall be in accordance with ASME B16.10, unless otherwise specified by the purchaser."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.3.3 Body Seats"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "5.3.3.1 The inside diameter of the seat opening shall not be less than that specified below:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— for all Classes in sizes up to DN 600 (NPS 24), ASME B16.34;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— for Classes 150 and 300 in sizes DN 650 to DN 750 (NPS 26 to NPS 30), ASME B16.34;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— for Classes 150 and 300 in sizes DN 800 to 1050 (NPS 32 to NPS 42), refer to Table 3."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "5.3.3.2 Seating surfaces shall be integral or faced with weld metal. Finished thickness of any facing material shall be not less than 1.6 mm (0.06 in.)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "5.3.3.3 Integral body seats are permitted in austenitic stainless steel valves. When an austenitic stainless steel or a hardfacing material is used for the body seat, this material may be weld-deposited directly on the valve body."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "5.3.3.4 Where separate seat rings are provided, they shall be shoulder or bottom seated, and either threaded or seal welded in place, except that for DN ≤ 50 (NPS ≤ 2) rolled or pressed in seat rings may be used. Threaded seat rings shall be seal welded; tack welding or stitch welding is not permitted."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "5.3.3.5 Body seat rings shall have adequate seating area surface and shall have edges equipped with a radius or chamfer as necessary, to prevent galling or any other damage to the disc when the valve is operated against pressure."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "5.3.3.6 Sealing compounds or greases shall not be used when assembling seat rings; however, a light lubricant having a viscosity no greater than kerosene may be used to prevent galling of mating threaded surfaces."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Table 3—Minimum Inside Diameter for Sizes DN 800 to DN 1050 (NPS 32 to NPS 42) in Classes 150 and 300",
      "columns": [
        "DN",
        "NPS",
        "Classes 150 and 300",
        "Classes 150 and 300"
      ],
      "rows": [
        {
          "DN": "800",
          "NPS": "32",
          "Classes 150 and 300": "779",
          "Classes 150 and 300": "30.67"
