{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.4 Bonnet"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.4.1 When designing the stem, gland, lantern ring (if supplied) and backseat, the manufacturer shall take into account stem guiding and the prevention of packing extrusion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.4.2 The bonnet shall include a conical or spherical stem backseat in one of the following forms:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— a bushing positively secured against coming loose, i.e. not relying on friction;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— an integral surface in the case of an austenitic stainless steel valve;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— an austenitic stainless steel or hardfaced weld deposit that is a minimum of 1.6 mm (0.06 in.) thick."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.4.3 Bonnets shall be one-piece castings or forgings."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.4.4 The gland bolting shall be secured to the bonnet so that the bolting is retained during repacking. When eyebolts are used, the eyebolt pin shall be anchored on both sides of the eyebolt. The anchors shall not include open slotted holes or be attached by fillet welds."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.4.5 Tapped test openings shall be provided only if specified in the purchase order."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.5 Bonnet-to-body Joint"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.5.1 The bonnet-to-body joint shall be a flange and gasket type."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.5.2 For Class 150 valves, the bonnet-to-body joint shall be one of the following types illustrated in ASME B16.5:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— flat face;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— raised face;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— tongue and groove;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— spigot and recess (i.e. male and female);"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— ring joint."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.5.3 For valves having pressure class designation Class > 150, the bonnet-to-body joint shall be as in 5.5.2, except that the flat face joint is not permitted."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "5.5.4 The bonnet flange gasket shall be one of the following:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— solid metal, corrugated or grooved (profiled) metal gasket with graphite filler;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— metal ring joint;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— spiral wound metal gasket with filler and a centering/compression ring;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— spiral wound metal gasket with filler but without a centering/compression ring, to be used only in ‘tongue and groove’ or ‘spigot and recess’ joints that prevent the gasket from unwinding and from buckling damage."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.5.5 Except for Class 150, the gasket shall not extend beyond the inner edge of the bolt holes."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.5.6 Except for Class 150 valves and valves in sizes DN 65 (NPS 2\u00bd) and smaller, bonnet-to-body flanges shall be circular."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.5.7 Bonnet and body flange nut bearing surfaces shall be parallel to the flange face within \u00b11\u00b0. Spot facing or back-facing required to meet the parallelism requirement shall be in accordance with ASME B16.5."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.5.8 The bonnet-to-body joint shall be secured by a minimum of four through type stud bolts. The minimum stud bolt size for each valve size shall be as follows:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— M10 or 3/8 when 25 ≤ DN ≤ 65 (1 ≤ NPS ≤ 2\u00bd);"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— M12 or 1/2 when 80 ≤ DN ≤ 200 (3 ≤ NPS ≤ 8);"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "— M16 or 5/8 when DN ≥ 250 (NPS ≥ 10)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.5.9 The total cross-sectional area of the bolts in valve bonnet bolting shall be in accordance with the requirements of ASME B16.34."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.5.10 At assembly, gasket contact surfaces shall be free of sealing compounds. A light coating of a lubricant, no heavier than kerosene, may be applied if needed to assist in proper gasket assembly."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.5.11 If pressure seal bonnet design is specified, the bonnet joint construction shall be in accordance with MSS SP-144 Style B, unless otherwise specified by the purchaser."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "5.6 Gate"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.6.1 Gate configurations are categorized as illustrated in Figure 2."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.6.1.1 A one-piece wedge gate—as either a solid or flexible wedge design—shall be furnished, unless otherwise specified by the purchaser."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.6.1.2 A two-piece split wedge gate or parallel seat double-disc gate may be furnished when specified by the purchaser. A split wedge gate consists of two independent seating parts that conform to the body seats when closed. The split wedge shall be designed so that the pieces cannot become separated, regardless of the gate position or valve orientation. A double-disc gate has a spreading mechanism (i.e., a wedging device or spring) that forces the two parallel discs to the body seats when closed."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.6.2 Except for a double-disc gate, in the open position, the gate shall completely clear the valve seat openings."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.6.3 The body and gate shall have guide surfaces to minimize wear of the gate seats during operation of the valve, to accurately position the gate throughout the travel distance to its seat, and to ensure the alignment of the gate and stem in all orientations without gate binding or galling. For sizes DN 650 (NPS 26) and above, as a minimum, wedge guides and body guides shall be hardfaced and machined with appropriate tolerances and clearances to allow for"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Wedge Gate"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "proper valve operation in any orientation, including effects of wear or galling. Wedge guides and/or body guides shall not protrude beyond the seat rings into the port area of the valve. The manufacturer shall provide in their installation and operation manual any operational limitations as a result of stem and valve orientation."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.6.4"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Gate seating surfaces shall be integral or faced with weld metal. Unless specified, hardfaced seating surfaces are not required. Finished thickness of any facing material shall be not less than 1.6 mm (0.06 in.)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.6.5"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Wedge gates shall be designed to account for seat wear. The dimensions that fix the position of the gate seats relative to the body seats shall be such that the gate, starting from the time of manufacture, can, as a result of seat wear, move into the seats by a distance, h, defined as wear travel. Wear travel is in a direction that is parallel with the valve stem (see Figure 3). The required minimum wear travel varies with valve size in accordance with Table 4."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "5.7 Yoke"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.7.1"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The yoke may be either an integral part of the bonnet or a separate part. The yoke shall retain the stem nut which links the handwheel to the stem."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.7.2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The yoke and stem nut assembly design shall permit stem nut removal while the valve is under pressure and backseated."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "5.7.3"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Yokes that are separate shall have yoke-to-bonnet mating surfaces machined so as to assure a proper bearing assembly interface."
    }
  ]
}

